From: Reindert Pelsma <reindert.pelsma@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 21 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] ui/sdl2: show the guest's GPU head

*** RUN ON REAL HARDWARE, ONCE.  What was measured and what was not is
*** under "WHAT THE HARDWARE SAID" below; read that before trusting any
*** of this.  The short version: the dma-buf reaches SDL's window and the
*** guest is usable, but the last link -- the pixels themselves -- was
*** confirmed by a person looking at the screen and by nothing
*** reproducible.  It stays on gpu-untested for that reason, and because
*** the same session reported the result as "works, but slow" (see "IF IT
*** IS SLOW").

`-display sdl,gl=on` never showed the guest.  With the default consoles the
window showed the black boot VGA; with `-vga none` it showed
"Guest has not initialized the display (yet)".  GTK shows the same guest fine
(patches/0005), so this is an SDL-side gap, and it matters because SDL is the
only backend that can take a real pointer lock on a Wayland host --
ui/sdl2.c:sdl_hide_cursor() calls SDL_SetRelativeMouseMode(), which SDL2
implements with zwp_pointer_constraints_v1 + zwp_relative_pointer_manager_v1,
while ui/gtk.c's gd_grab_pointer() does gdk_seat_grab() plus a warp and asks
the compositor for no lock at all.

WHAT IT IS NOT.  The obvious theory is that nvkvm's console is registered too
late: sdl2_display_init() counts consoles once, allocates one sdl2_console per
console found, and never grows that array, so a console that appears afterwards
would have no listener and no window.  That is not what happens here.
nvkvm registers its console from virtio_nvgpu_device_realize(), i.e. from
qemu_create_cli_devices(), and system/vl.c runs that BEFORE qemu_init_displays()
-- and before phase PHASE_MACHINE_READY, so qemu_console_register()'s
"graphical consoles before text consoles" path applies and the head is console 1
(console 0 with `-vga none`).  SDL does create a listener for it.  patches/0005
depends on the same ordering from the other side: GTK's notebook has a page for
that console, which it could not if the console arrived later.  Ruled out by
reading the code, not by testing -- but the code is unambiguous.

WHAT IT ACTUALLY IS.  Three things, all on the GL scanout path:

  1. ui/sdl2.c's dcl_gl_ops has dpy_gl_scanout_texture but NO
     dpy_gl_scanout_dmabuf.  dpy_gl_scanout_dmabuf() in ui/console.c is
     `if (dcl->ops->dpy_gl_scanout_dmabuf)` -- a listener without the callback
     is SKIPPED, no warning, no error.  nvkvm presents by dma-buf
     (nvkvm_present_gl() -> dpy_gl_scanout_dmabuf + dpy_gl_update), so every
     frame was dropped inside QEMU before it reached SDL.  The dpy_gl_update
     that follows lands in sdl2_gl_scanout_flush(), which returns immediately
     because !scon->scanout_mode -- scanout mode is set by the texture path,
     which was never reached.  ui/gtk.c has gd_egl_scanout_dmabuf and
     gd_gl_area_scanout_dmabuf; SDL had neither.  This is the whole reason the
     window kept showing whatever surface was last installed.

  2. Nothing on the scanout path creates a window.  Windows are created in
     sdl2_gl_switch()/sdl2_2d_switch(), i.e. only when a SURFACE arrives, and
     for a console above index 0 those functions actively destroy the window
     while the surface is the placeholder.  A console whose content is a GL
     scanout and nothing else therefore has real_window == NULL for ever, and
     sdl2_gl_scanout_texture() then calls SDL_GL_MakeCurrent(NULL, NULL) and
     sdl2_gl_scanout_flush() would call SDL_GetWindowSize(NULL, &ww, &wh) and
     blit with an uninitialised size.  patches/0005 learned the same lesson for
     GTK: the GL entry points are a separate path from the surface one and have
     to be hooked separately, or a fix works in whichever present mode happens
     to be under test and silently does nothing in the other.

  3. egl_dmabuf_import_texture() imports into the global `qemu_egl_display`,
     and nothing in ui/sdl2*.c ever set it -- SDL creates its own GL context
     and QEMU's qemu_egl_init_dpy_*() path is never taken, so the global was
     either EGL_NO_DISPLAY or, worse, whatever nvkvm's readback path left there
     when it called egl_init() on the render node.  Upstream already solves
     this for GTK's GtkGLArea in gl_area_realize(): make the context current,
     then read the display back out of it with eglGetCurrentDisplay().  Same
     three lines here, in sdl2_gl_bind_window_egl(), called from
     sdl2_window_create() where SDL_GL_CreateContext() has just left the new
     context current.

WHAT IT DOES.  Adds sdl2_gl_scanout_dmabuf() -- gd_egl_scanout_dmabuf() with
the GTK parts removed: import, then hand the texture to the texture path that
already worked -- plus sdl2_gl_release_dmabuf() (without it every flip abandons
a texture and its EGLImage, the leak gd_gl_release_dmabuf() exists to stop) and
sdl2_gl_has_dmabuf(), so a window whose backend is GLX rather than EGL reports
no dma-buf support instead of claiming it and drawing nothing.  Window creation
moves into a helper the scanout path calls as well; it also initialises the
console's shader, because surface_gl_create_texture() asserts on a NULL one and
leaving scanout mode goes straight there.

AND ONE POLICY BIT, the analogue of patches/0005.  SDL gives every console its
own window rather than a notebook page, so there is no tab to switch; the
equivalent is to raise the guest's window the first time it has real content.
This is not tidiness.  Ctrl+Alt+G grabs WHICHEVER window has focus, so with the
boot VGA in front the grab -- and every keystroke and mouse delta after it --
goes to console 0 and the guest never sees any of it.  The latch is 0005's,
term for term: only a console above the boot console, only once, only if the
user has not hidden it, and it logs the one line that explains a window moving
on its own.  With `-vga none` the guest IS console 0 and it is a no-op.

WHY NOT JUST RECOMMEND `-vga none`.  It would make several things line up --
one window, and ui/sdl2.c's several hardcoded references to sdl2_console[0]
(sdl_mouse_mode_change(), the fullscreen startup grab) would then mean the
guest.  It is still the wrong answer: scripts/run_test_vm.sh documents why the
VGA is there, which is that GRUB and the early kernel have nothing to draw on
without it and boot stalls in gfxterm video init with no console to say so.
Deleting the boot display to work around a display bug trades a visible problem
for an invisible one.  The multi-console case is handled instead.

Only compiled into a build configured --enable-sdl, i.e. NVKVM_QEMU_UI=1.  The
default headless build applies this patch and never compiles a line of it.  The
GTK path is untouched: no file this patch edits is compiled into a GTK-only
build.

HOW TO ACTUALLY VERIFY, on a machine with an NVIDIA GPU and a display:

    NVKVM_QEMU_UI=1 scripts/build_qemu.sh --force
    VM_DISPLAY="sdl,gl=on" VM_SERIAL=none scripts/run_test_vm.sh

  * expect one line on stderr, once: "nvkvm: guest display is live -- raised
    SDL window for console 1", and a second QEMU window in front showing the
    guest desktop.  `grep -c` over the whole run must be 1.
  * with NVKVM_PRESENT_MODE=gl (the zero-copy path this patch is really about)
    the same must hold, and "nvkvm present: window mode=GL-zerocopy" must
    appear.  With NVKVM_PRESENT_MODE=readback it must also hold -- that path
    went through the surface entry points and is the one thing here that could
    already have worked, so it is the regression check.
  * `-vga none`: no raise line at all, one window, the desktop still appears in
    it.
  * GTK must be unchanged: VM_DISPLAY="gtk,gl=on" still shows the desktop and
    still logs 0005's "switched window to console page 1" exactly once.

WHAT THE HARDWARE SAID.  One session on an RTX 4070, driver 595.84, host
session GNOME on WAYLAND, Mint 22.3 guest, `-display sdl,gl=on`.

MEASURED:
  * builds: NVKVM_QEMU_UI=1 scripts/build_qemu.sh --force ran end to end, the
    patch log reads "applied: 0008-sdl2-show-the-guest-gpu-head.patch", and
    ui_sdl2.c.o, ui_sdl2-gl.c.o, ui_sdl2-2d.c.o and ui_sdl2-input.c.o all
    compiled with no warning or error mentioning sdl2;
  * NVKVM_PRESENT_MODE=gl -- the dma-buf path this patch is really about --
    logs "nvkvm present: window mode=GL-zerocopy (console_has_gl=1)" and then
    "nvkvm: guest display is live -- raised SDL window for console 1".  That
    second line is emitted at the END of sdl2_gl_scanout_texture(), and in GL
    mode that function is reachable ONLY from the new sdl2_gl_scanout_dmabuf()
    and only after egl_dmabuf_import_texture() returned a non-zero texture.  So
    the guest's dma-buf really was imported on the window's own EGL display and
    installed as the scanout.  Before this patch dpy_gl_scanout_dmabuf() had no
    callback to call at all and the frame was discarded inside ui/console.c;
  * it fires exactly once: `grep -c` over the whole run is 1;
  * zero lines matching "error" in the run -- in particular no
    "eglCreateImageKHR failed", which is what a failed import prints;
  * SDL was a genuine Wayland client: the QEMU process held a
    /memfd:wayland-cursor fd, which only libwayland-cursor creates.  GTK on
    this same host held zero wayland references, which is the whole reason
    this backend is worth having;
  * NVKVM_PRESENT_MODE=readback -- the surface path, the one that could
    already have worked and therefore the regression check for this patch --
    also logs the raise line exactly once with no errors, and
    NVKVM_PRESENT_DUMP wrote a 1920x1080 frame that IS the live Cinnamon
    desktop (fetched and looked at).  So the console SDL is bound to carries a
    real desktop, and this patch did not break the path that already worked;
  * SDL_VIDEODRIVER=x11 SDL_VIDEO_X11_FORCE_EGL=1 reaches the same two log
    lines, so none of this is Wayland-specific.

NOT MEASURED, and the gap is exactly where you would want it not to be:
  * nothing reproducible ever read the pixels out of the SDL window.  A
    TEMPORARY debug hook (not part of this patch, and not in this diff) was
    added to sdl2_gl_scanout_flush() to glReadPixels the back buffer straight
    after egl_fb_blit().  It logged "window readback 1920x1080" repeatedly and
    wrote a 6,220,817-byte PPM -- exactly 1920x1080x3 plus header -- and the
    machine was returned before that file could be fetched.  So the blit ran,
    onto a correctly sized target, and its output was never seen;
  * the person at that console reported the guest usable and pointer lock
    working.  That is the only evidence the window shows the desktop.  It is a
    report, not a measurement, and it is written down here as one.

IF IT IS SLOW, and that session said it was, look in this order.  None of
these is a line of code in this patch; the patch only decides whether frames
arrive at all.

  1. WHICH PRESENT MODE ARE YOU IN.  nvkvm_present_decide_mode() defaults to
     READBACK unless NVKVM_PRESENT_MODE=gl is in the environment -- it will not
     guess GL from the render node, deliberately.  Readback does, per frame,
     glReadPixels of 1920x1080x4 out of the GPU into a CPU DisplaySurface and
     then surface_gl_update_texture() to push the same 8 MB back in, plus an
     eglMakeCurrent onto a different EGL display and back.  Two 8 MB copies and
     a full pipeline stall every frame is a textbook "works but sluggish".
     CHECK: grep "window mode" in the QEMU log.  If it says readback, relaunch
     with NVKVM_PRESENT_MODE=gl and compare.  This is the first thing to try
     and costs nothing.
  2. PER-FRAME DMA-BUF IMPORT.  nvkvm_present_gl() builds a FRESH QemuDmaBuf
     every frame, so egl_dmabuf_import_texture() never hits its
     `texture != 0` early-out: every frame is eglCreateImageKHR +
     glGenTextures + glEGLImageTargetTexStorageEXT + eglDestroyImageKHR, and
     sdl2_gl_release_dmabuf() then glDeleteTextures the previous one.  nvkvm's
     own readback path has a comment saying this exact churn "reliably killed
     the NVIDIA driver: SIGSEGV inside libnvidia-eglcore ... within ~2 s", and
     it carries an 8-entry import cache to avoid it.  The zero-copy path has
     no such cache because it delegates the import to the UI.  ui/gtk.c has
     the identical shape, so this is not SDL's fault -- but SDL runs with
     SDL_GL_SetSwapInterval(0), i.e. unthrottled, so it will hit the churn
     harder.  CHECK: NVKVM_DISP_STATS=1 prints frames/s and present_mean ms.
     FIX SHAPE: hand the UI a STABLE QemuDmaBuf per guest scanout bo, so the
     import early-out fires.  nvkvm already has the keying for it
     (nvkvm_present_cached_tex, keyed on owner + gem handle + geometry).  That
     fix belongs in hw/misc/nvkvm_present_egl.c and helps GTK too.
  3. INPUT LATENCY, if "slow" means the mouse feels heavy rather than the
     picture stuttering.  sdl2_poll_events() runs ONLY from the DCL refresh
     callback, and ui/sdl2.c flips that callback's update_interval between
     SDL2_REFRESH_INTERVAL_BUSY (10 ms) and GUI_REFRESH_INTERVAL_DEFAULT
     (30 ms) on an idle counter.  So relative motion is drained at 33-100 Hz
     in bursts, whatever rate the compositor delivers it at.  CHECK: evtest in
     the guest while moving the mouse steadily -- deltas arriving in clumps
     every 10 or 30 ms is this; smooth ~1 ms spacing is not.
  4. NOT this patch's per-frame cost.  What 0008 adds to each frame is a
     pointer test (the ensure-window check), two assignments (the
     qemu_egl_display borrow) and one already-false branch
     (nvkvm_sdl2_maybe_raise after it has fired).  The one wasteful line is
     the SDL_GL_MakeCurrent in sdl2_gl_release_dmabuf(), which is redundant
     because the context is already current -- worth deleting for tidiness,
     not a cause of visible slowness.

NOT UPSTREAMABLE AS WRITTEN.  The three gaps listed under WHAT IT ACTUALLY IS
are plain upstream gaps and the fixes for them belong in qemu-devel roughly as
they stand -- ui/sdl2.c is the only remaining front end with a GL context and
no dma-buf scanout.  The raise
is nvkvm policy ("the interesting console is the one that is not the boot VGA")
hardcoded into a generic front end, with an nvkvm-branded symbol and log line,
and would have to become the same dpy_console_request_focus()-shaped mechanism
patches/0005 wants before either could go upstream.
---
 include/ui/sdl2.h |  51 +++++++++++++++
 ui/sdl2-2d.c      |   3 +
 ui/sdl2-gl.c      | 192 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 ui/sdl2.c         |  15 +++++
 4 files changed, 261 insertions(+)

diff --git a/include/ui/sdl2.h b/include/ui/sdl2.h
index dbe6e3d..cce22b1 100644
--- a/include/ui/sdl2.h
+++ b/include/ui/sdl2.h
@@ -51,9 +51,50 @@ struct sdl2_console {
     egl_fb win_fb;
     bool y0_top;
     bool scanout_mode;
+    /*
+     * nvkvm: the EGL display THIS window renders on, read out of its own GL
+     * context (sdl2_gl_bind_window_egl()).  Kept per console rather than in
+     * the global qemu_egl_display, which other code owns -- see the comment on
+     * sdl2_gl_bind_window_egl().  EGL_NO_DISPLAY when SDL chose a non-EGL
+     * backend, e.g. GLX on X11.
+     */
+    EGLDisplay edpy;
+    /* Can that display import a dma-buf?  Reported to ui/console.c through
+     * .dpy_has_dmabuf, so a console that requires dma-buf is not handed a
+     * window that cannot show it. */
+    bool has_dmabuf;
 #endif
 };
 
+/*
+ * nvkvm: one-shot raise of the guest's head.  GTK has tabs and patches/0005
+ * switches to the right one; SDL gives every console its own window, so the
+ * equivalent is to bring the guest's window to the front the first time it has
+ * something to show.  It matters for more than tidiness: Ctrl+Alt+G grabs
+ * WHICHEVER window has focus, so with the boot VGA in front the grab -- and
+ * every key and delta after it -- lands on console 0 instead of the guest.
+ *
+ * Latch, not a policy engine, exactly as in 0005: fire only for a console above
+ * the boot console, only when it has a window, only when the user has not
+ * hidden it, and at most once.  With -vga none the guest IS console 0 and this
+ * is a no-op, which is correct -- there is only one window.
+ */
+extern bool nvkvm_sdl2_raised;
+
+static inline void nvkvm_sdl2_maybe_raise(struct sdl2_console *scon)
+{
+    if (nvkvm_sdl2_raised || !scon || scon->idx == 0 ||
+        !scon->real_window || scon->hidden) {
+        return;
+    }
+    nvkvm_sdl2_raised = true;
+    SDL_RaiseWindow(scon->real_window);
+    /* One line, once: a window jumping to the front is surprising if you are
+     * not expecting it, and this is the only record that it happened. */
+    fprintf(stderr, "nvkvm: guest display is live -- raised SDL window for "
+                    "console %d\n", scon->idx);
+}
+
 void sdl2_window_create(struct sdl2_console *scon);
 void sdl2_window_destroy(struct sdl2_console *scon);
 void sdl2_window_resize(struct sdl2_console *scon);
@@ -97,4 +138,14 @@ void sdl2_gl_scanout_texture(DisplayChangeListener *dcl,
 void sdl2_gl_scanout_flush(DisplayChangeListener *dcl,
                            uint32_t x, uint32_t y, uint32_t w, uint32_t h);
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_OPENGL
+/* nvkvm: dma-buf scanout for SDL -- see patches/0008 for why it was missing. */
+void sdl2_gl_bind_window_egl(struct sdl2_console *scon);
+void sdl2_gl_scanout_dmabuf(DisplayChangeListener *dcl,
+                            QemuDmaBuf *dmabuf);
+void sdl2_gl_release_dmabuf(DisplayChangeListener *dcl,
+                            QemuDmaBuf *dmabuf);
+bool sdl2_gl_has_dmabuf(DisplayChangeListener *dcl);
+#endif
+
 #endif /* SDL2_H */
diff --git a/ui/sdl2-2d.c b/ui/sdl2-2d.c
index 7305238..ff97a59 100644
--- a/ui/sdl2-2d.c
+++ b/ui/sdl2-2d.c
@@ -122,6 +122,9 @@ void sdl2_2d_switch(DisplayChangeListener *dcl,
                                       surface_width(new_surface),
                                       surface_height(new_surface));
     sdl2_2d_redraw(scon);
+
+    nvkvm_sdl2_maybe_raise(scon);   /* nvkvm: past the placeholder check above,
+                                     * so this is real content */
 }
 
 void sdl2_2d_refresh(DisplayChangeListener *dcl)
diff --git a/ui/sdl2-gl.c b/ui/sdl2-gl.c
index e01d9ab..87697d8 100644
--- a/ui/sdl2-gl.c
+++ b/ui/sdl2-gl.c
@@ -46,6 +46,86 @@ static void sdl2_set_scanout_mode(struct sdl2_console *scon, bool scanout)
     }
 }
 
+/*
+ * nvkvm: learn which EGL display this window renders on.
+ *
+ * egl_dmabuf_import_texture() imports into the global `qemu_egl_display`, and
+ * nothing in ui/sdl2*.c ever set it -- SDL creates its own GL context and
+ * QEMU's qemu_egl_init_dpy_*() path is never taken, so the import had no
+ * display to work on.  Upstream solves the same problem for GTK's GtkGLArea in
+ * gl_area_realize(): make the context current, then read the display back out
+ * of it.  SDL_GL_CreateContext() has just left the new context current on this
+ * thread, so eglGetCurrentDisplay() names it here too.
+ *
+ * WHERE THIS DIVERGES FROM gl_area_realize(), and it is not a style choice:
+ * that function assigns straight to the global.  We must not.  The global is
+ * ALREADY OWNED by something else in an nvkvm run -- hw/misc/nvkvm_present_egl.c
+ * calls egl_init() on the NVIDIA render node and then does
+ * eglMakeCurrent(qemu_egl_display, ..., qemu_egl_rn_ctx) on every readback
+ * frame.  Overwriting the global from here makes that pairing invalid on the
+ * NEXT frame (a context does not belong to another display), eglMakeCurrent
+ * fails, and the readback path -- which is nvkvm's DEFAULT present mode, and
+ * the one that already worked under SDL -- stops presenting silently.  So the
+ * window's display is kept on the console and the global is borrowed only for
+ * the duration of an import, in sdl2_gl_scanout_dmabuf().
+ *
+ * If SDL chose a non-EGL backend (GLX on X11) eglGetCurrentDisplay() returns
+ * EGL_NO_DISPLAY and this window truthfully reports no dma-buf support, rather
+ * than claiming it and then drawing nothing.
+ */
+void sdl2_gl_bind_window_egl(struct sdl2_console *scon)
+{
+    scon->edpy = EGL_NO_DISPLAY;
+    scon->has_dmabuf = false;
+#ifdef CONFIG_GBM
+    if (!scon->winctx) {
+        return;
+    }
+    scon->edpy = eglGetCurrentDisplay();
+    if (scon->edpy == EGL_NO_DISPLAY) {
+        return;
+    }
+    /* qemu_egl_has_dmabuf() asks the global; ask ours. */
+    scon->has_dmabuf = epoxy_has_egl_extension(scon->edpy,
+                                               "EGL_EXT_image_dma_buf_import");
+#endif
+}
+
+/*
+ * nvkvm: make sure this console has a window before scanning out to it.
+ *
+ * Windows are created in sdl2_gl_switch()/sdl2_2d_switch(), i.e. only on the
+ * SURFACE path, and for a console above index 0 that path actively DESTROYS
+ * the window while the surface is the placeholder.  A console whose content
+ * arrives as a GL scanout and nothing else therefore never gets a window at
+ * all, and every draw below silently no-ops on a NULL SDL_Window.  Same lesson
+ * as patches/0005 for GTK: the GL entry points are a separate path and have to
+ * be hooked separately, or the fix works in whichever present mode happens to
+ * be under test and does nothing in the other.
+ *
+ * Returns false when there is still nothing to size a window by, which is not
+ * an error -- the next scanout tries again.
+ */
+static bool sdl2_gl_ensure_window(struct sdl2_console *scon)
+{
+    if (scon->real_window) {
+        return true;
+    }
+    sdl2_window_create(scon);
+    if (!scon->real_window) {
+        return false;
+    }
+    if (!scon->gls) {
+        /*
+         * surface_gl_create_texture() asserts on a NULL shader, and leaving
+         * scanout mode goes straight there.  The surface path pairs the shader
+         * with the window; so does this one.
+         */
+        scon->gls = qemu_gl_init_shader();
+    }
+    return true;
+}
+
 static void sdl2_gl_render_surface(struct sdl2_console *scon)
 {
     int ww, wh;
@@ -106,6 +186,9 @@ void sdl2_gl_switch(DisplayChangeListener *dcl,
     }
 
     surface_gl_create_texture(scon->gls, scon->surface);
+
+    nvkvm_sdl2_maybe_raise(scon);   /* nvkvm: past the placeholder check above,
+                                     * so this is real content */
 }
 
 void sdl2_gl_refresh(DisplayChangeListener *dcl)
@@ -211,6 +294,11 @@ void sdl2_gl_scanout_texture(DisplayChangeListener *dcl,
     struct sdl2_console *scon = container_of(dcl, struct sdl2_console, dcl);
 
     assert(scon->opengl);
+
+    if (!sdl2_gl_ensure_window(scon)) {   /* nvkvm: a scanout is real content */
+        return;
+    }
+
     scon->x = x;
     scon->y = y;
     scon->w = w;
@@ -222,6 +310,105 @@ void sdl2_gl_scanout_texture(DisplayChangeListener *dcl,
     sdl2_set_scanout_mode(scon, true);
     egl_fb_setup_for_tex(&scon->guest_fb, backing_width, backing_height,
                          backing_id, false);
+
+    nvkvm_sdl2_maybe_raise(scon);
+}
+
+/*
+ * nvkvm: import a dma-buf scanout and present it.
+ *
+ * SDL's DisplayChangeListenerOps carried dpy_gl_scanout_texture but NOT
+ * dpy_gl_scanout_dmabuf, and dpy_gl_scanout_dmabuf() in ui/console.c skips
+ * every listener that lacks the callback -- silently.  A device that presents
+ * by dma-buf (virtio-gpu with udmabuf, and nvkvm's GL zero-copy path) therefore
+ * had its frames dropped on the floor under -display sdl, and the window went
+ * on showing whatever surface was last installed: the boot VGA, or the
+ * "Guest has not initialized the display (yet)" placeholder.
+ *
+ * This is gd_egl_scanout_dmabuf() with the GTK parts removed: import, then
+ * hand the resulting texture to the texture path that already worked.
+ */
+void sdl2_gl_scanout_dmabuf(DisplayChangeListener *dcl,
+                            QemuDmaBuf *dmabuf)
+{
+#ifdef CONFIG_GBM
+    struct sdl2_console *scon = container_of(dcl, struct sdl2_console, dcl);
+    uint32_t x, y, width, height, backing_width, backing_height, texture;
+    EGLDisplay saved_dpy;
+    bool y0_top;
+
+    assert(scon->opengl);
+
+    if (!sdl2_gl_ensure_window(scon) || scon->edpy == EGL_NO_DISPLAY) {
+        return;
+    }
+    /* The import binds a texture into whatever context is current. */
+    SDL_GL_MakeCurrent(scon->real_window, scon->winctx);
+
+    /*
+     * Borrow the global for the length of the import and put it back.  See
